      "content": "You are absolutely right that we must distinguish between legitimate policy critique and weaponized insults, as teaching kids to spot the difference is the only way to build real critical thinking skills. Legitimate critique uses terms like \"regressive\" or \"stubborn\" to highlight specific flaws in a policy or argument, whereas weaponized language uses them purely to shut down debate and avoid addressing the actual substance of the disagreement. To teach this effectively, you should show students how to look for evidence: if the speaker offers data or a logical reason for their label, it is a critique; if they immediately attack the person or refuse to engage with the facts, it is a tactic to silence opposition. This distinction empowers students to identify when they are being genuinely challenged versus when they are being unfairly silenced by bad-faith actors.",
